# Emphasis

@lede
Four kinds. One marker per side. No exceptions.
@end

## The four markers

@t
| Construct | Source | Renders as
| Bold | `*text*` | `<strong>`
| Italic | `_text_` | `<em>`
| Underline | `+text+` | `<u>`
| Strikethrough | `~text~` | `<s>`

Each marker is a single character on each side. Doubled markers
(`**bold**`, `__italic__`) are *not* valid — they are @ec[B0206] errors.

## Why singles only

Markdown's `**bold**` versus `*italic*` distinction is the single largest
source of "what does this Markdown actually mean" bugs. Brief sidesteps it
by giving each emphasis kind its own dedicated marker. There is no
"strength" hierarchy; bold is `*text*`, end of story.

## Nesting rules

Different kinds nest freely:

```brief
*bold with _italic inside_ and back to bold*
```

The same kind cannot nest:

```brief
*outer *inner* outer*    // B0204 — same marker re-used
```

Multi-line emphasis is also an error (@ec[B0205]). An emphasis run must
open and close on the same source line.

## Word-boundary rules

A marker flanked by alphanumerics on *both* sides is treated as a literal
character, not as emphasis:

```brief
snake_case_name              // literal — no italic
file_with_underscores.txt    // literal
3*4 = 12                     // literal asterisks
```

This is why everyday code identifiers don't accidentally become italic.

## Escaping

To force a marker to be literal in a position where it would otherwise be
emphasis, escape it with a backslash:

```brief
This costs \*5\* dollars.
```

## In LLM mode

Emphasis markers are preserved by default. The flag `--strip-emphasis`
removes them entirely if you are tight on tokens and the LLM does not need
the structural hint:

```bash
brief compile doc.brf --target=llm --strip-emphasis
```

## Inline code is not emphasis

A backticked region (`` `like this` ``) is verbatim. Markers inside an
inline code span are literal. See @ref[language/inline-code.brf](Inline code).
